---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '@/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Ayush Dev | Computer Engineering student"
  description="Ayush Dev is a Computer Engineering student from Nepal who keeps exploring, building, playing, and learning."
>
  <article class="minimal-home">
    <header class="profile-header">
      <img class="profile-image" src="/images/devad.png" width="392" height="359" alt="Ayush Dev" />
      <div>
        <h1>Ayush Dev</h1>
        <p>not the developer Dev, just Dev as my surname. Pretty interesting, right?</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="profile-links" aria-label="Ayush Dev online">
      <a href={site.socials[0].href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="GitHub" title="GitHub">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2a10 10 0 0 0-3.16 19.49c.5.09.68-.22.68-.48v-1.87c-2.78.6-3.37-1.18-3.37-1.18-.45-1.16-1.11-1.47-1.11-1.47-.91-.62.07-.61.07-.61 1 .07 1.53 1.03 1.53 1.03.9 1.53 2.35 1.09 2.92.83.09-.65.35-1.09.64-1.34-2.22-.25-4.55-1.11-4.55-4.94 0-1.09.39-1.98 1.03-2.68-.1-.25-.45-1.27.1-2.64 0 0 .84-.27 2.75 1.02A9.58 9.58 0 0 1 12 6.82c.85 0 1.71.11 2.51.34 1.91-1.29 2.75-1.02 2.75-1.02.55 1.37.2 2.39.1 2.64.64.7 1.03 1.59 1.03 2.68 0 3.84-2.34 4.68-4.57 4.93.36.31.68.92.68 1.86v2.76c0 .27.18.58.69.48A10 10 0 0 0 12 2Z" /></svg>
      </a>
      <a href={site.socials[1].href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="LinkedIn" title="LinkedIn">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6.5 8.2H3.2V21h3.3V8.2ZM4.85 3A1.93 1.93 0 1 0 4.85 6.86 1.93 1.93 0 0 0 4.85 3ZM21 13.66c0-3.86-2.06-5.66-4.81-5.66a4.15 4.15 0 0 0-3.75 2.06V8.2H9.15V21h3.29v-6.34c0-1.67.32-3.29 2.39-3.29 2.04 0 2.06 1.91 2.06 3.4V21H21v-7.34Z" /></svg>
      </a>
      <a href={site.socials[2].href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="X" title="X">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18.9 2H22l-6.77 7.74L23.2 22h-6.24l-4.89-6.39L6.48 22H3.36l7.26-8.3L2.98 2h6.4l4.42 5.84L18.9 2Zm-1.1 17.84h1.72L8.44 4.05H6.59L17.8 19.84Z" /></svg>
      </a>
      <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`} aria-label="Email" title="Email"><span aria-hidden="true">@</span></a>
      <span class="profile-link-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <a href="/journal/" aria-label="Writing" title="Writing">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 3h11a3 3 0 0 1 3 3v15H7a3 3 0 0 1-3-3V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1Zm2 16h10V6a1 1 0 0 0-1-1H6v12.17A3 3 0 0 1 7 17h8v2H7a1 1 0 0 0 0 2v-2Z" /></svg>
      </a>
      <a href={site.cv} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Résumé" title="Résumé">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 2h8l5 5v15H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2Zm7 2H6v16h11V8h-4V4Zm-4 8h5v2H9v-2Zm0 4h5v2H9v-2Z" /></svg>
      </a>
    </nav>

    <div class="about-copy">
      <p>I’m a Computer Engineering student from Nepal, and honestly, technology has been a part of me for as long as I can remember. Ever since I was a child, I was fascinated by the internet. I didn’t just use it; I wanted to understand it. I would wander through corners of the internet, discovering random things, reading about things I barely understood, finding facts that made me go, “Wait… this is actually possible?” When I joined my bachelor's, that curiosity only grew stronger. It felt like I had finally entered the world I had been looking at from the outside for years.</p>

      <h2>About</h2>

      <p>I started exploring operating systems. I talked to CS people and asked what they used. Someone would mention Linux, so I would install it. Someone would talk about a new tool, framework, language, database, or technology, and I would go home and try it myself. If something sounded even slightly interesting, I couldn't leave it alone. I had to dig deeper. I wanted to know how it worked, why people used it, what I could build with it, and whether I could make it run on my own machine. And somehow, that's how I've spent years learning.</p>

      <p>But there’s a sad part to it too. I've touched so many things, but I never truly mastered one. I've built projects in different domains. I've tried different technologies, explored different areas of computer science, jumped from one idea to another, and learned enough to understand a little bit of almost everything. But whenever I look around, I see people who have become really good at one thing. And sometimes I wonder… What did I become? A little bit of everything, but never enough of one thing to call myself an expert. Maybe that's why I call myself: Jack of all trades, master of none.</p>

      <p>And then there's gaming. I love games. I'm not a professional, and I've never been particularly good at one game. But I don't play because I want to be the best. I play because, in a strange way, it heals something in me. When I was a kid, I used to watch other people play games and wish I could experience them myself. I didn't have the kind of PC I have now. I could only watch and imagine what it would feel like. Now I finally have a good PC. So I play.</p>

      <p class="closing-note"><strong>Maybe I’m still searching for the answers<br />I couldn’t find at the back of the book.</strong></p>
      <p class="projects-cta">check out my projects if u wanna see. <a href="/projects/">See</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
